 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The article goes with the noun",
     "texto": [
      "In Spanish every noun is either masculine or feminine, and the article goes with it:\n\n- **el** libro, **un** libro (masculine)\n- **la** mesa, **una** mesa (feminine)\n- **los** libros, **las** mesas (plural)\n\nUsually words ending in **-o** are masculine and words ending in **-a** are feminine. But not always:\n\n- **el mapa**, **el problema** (they end in -a and are masculine)\n- **la mano** (it ends in -o and is feminine)\n\nAlso, feminine nouns that begin with a stressed **a** take **el**: **el agua**. But they are still feminine: **las aguas**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "El agua, la mano and el mapa",
     "texto": [
      "Some words trick you. Look at these three cases:\n\n- **el agua**: it is feminine, but it begins with a stressed «a» and that is why it takes **el**. In the plural it goes back to normal: **las aguas**.\n- **la mano**: it ends in -o, but it is feminine.\n- **el mapa** and **el problema**: they end in -a, but they are masculine.\n\nThe ending helps, but the article has the last word."
     ],
